  "image_name": "2f714e6805aef9c38c9f21b74fbf7a72.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes autophagy and highlights how this pathway is disrupted in several neurodegenerative diseases. Across the top, autophagy is shown progressing through three broad stages: initiation and cargo capture, expansion and closure of the membrane to form an autophagosome, and then maturation through fusion with endosomes and lysosomes to create an autolysosome where contents are degraded.\n\nBelow, the same pathway is overlaid with disease-linked proteins for ALS/FTD, Parkinson’s disease, and late-onset Alzheimer’s disease. In ALS/FTD, factors such as OPTN, p62, TBK1, UBQLN2, CHMP2B, VCP, GRN, FIG4, and proteins including TDP43, FUS, and SOD1 are positioned at different steps, suggesting defects in cargo recognition, autophagosome maturation, and lysosomal degradation. In Parkinson’s disease, the diagram emphasizes mitophagy, with PINK1, PRKN/PARK7, FBXO7, and mitochondrial cargo, while SNCA and LRRK2 appear at multiple stages, implying broader interference with autophagy and lysosomal processing. In late-onset Alzheimer’s disease, tau is linked to early cargo capture, and genes such as PICALM, BIN1, RIN3, CD2AP, APOE4, TREM2, PLD3, and GRN cluster around vesicle trafficking, fusion, and lysosomal steps. Overall, the figure conveys that diverse disease genes converge on different points of the autophagy–lysosome system.",
